A node affinity conflict occurs when a pod's node affinity rules cannot be satisfied by any existing node or provisionable node. The scheduler evaluates node labels against the pod's required affinity constraints, and if no match exists, the pod remains Pending with a "didn't match Pod's node affinity" error. In Karpenter environments, such conflicts can also occur when pod requirements exceed the constraints defined in NodePools or EC2NodeClasses, preventing node provisioning.

Karpenter creating nodes does not guarantee scheduling. The pod scheduler still validates node readiness, taints/tolerations, node selectors, affinities, available resources, PVC topology, and NodePool constraints. My first step is always kubectl describe pod and review the scheduling events. Then I verify node readiness, taints, resource availability, and NodeClaim status. In most production incidents I've seen, the root causes are untolerated taints, node selector mismatches, startup taints not being removed, or insufficient allocatable resources on the newly provisioned nodes.

